WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.670
You send a message. Time passes. There’s no reply.

2
00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.690
Before long, your mind starts filling in the silence.

3
00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.270
‘They’re upset with me.’

4
00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.310
‘I must have said something wrong.’

5
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:19.306
Those thoughts can feel convincing—especially when you’re anxious. But feeling

6
00:00:19.306 --> 00:00:22.803
certain and having evidence are different things.

7
00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:25.513
Here’s a useful distinction.

8
00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:29.293
‘They haven’t replied yet’ is something you can observe.

9
00:00:29.293 --> 00:00:34.777
‘They’re upset with me’ is one possible explanation. It hasn’t been confirmed.

10
00:00:34.777 --> 00:00:41.247
Try asking yourself two questions: What do I actually know? And what am I

11
00:00:41.247 --> 00:00:43.096
adding to the story?

12
00:00:43.516 --> 00:00:49.161
You don’t have to replace a worrying prediction with a cheerful one.

13
00:00:49.161 --> 00:00:53.866
‘Everything is definitely fine’ is also more than you know.

14
00:00:53.866 --> 00:01:00.150
A balanced response might be: ‘I haven’t heard back, and I don’t know why

15
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:00.599
yet.’

16
00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:05.749
Then consider whether anything needs your attention now—or whether you can

17
00:01:05.749 --> 00:01:08.759
return to another part of your day.

18
00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:14.368
Your feelings deserve care. Your predictions deserve a little room for

19
00:01:14.368 --> 00:01:14.878
questioning.

20
00:01:14.878 --> 00:01:20.421
You can take a thought seriously without treating it as a fact.
